BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:Discussion - Why Music is Genetic
DTSTART;TZID=America/Toronto:20261211T190000
DTEND;TZID=America/Toronto:20261211T210000
DTSTAMP:20261114T070218Z
UID:0f96a33d296e07a19a786341@montreal-poetry-calendar
CATEGORIES:Poetry & Literature
DESCRIPTION:Contributor(s): Bullerdiek\, Jörn (Author) \, Süßmuth\, Chr
 istine (Author) \, Grönemeyer\, Dietrich (Foreword by) \, Darwin\, Sarah 
 (Foreword by)\nWe are happy to welcome the author of Why Music is Genetic
 \, Jörn Bullerdiek for a discussion on the subject of genetics and music.
  \n\nThe presentation will be with live musical examples by Baroque compo
 sers John Dowland\, Francesca Caccini and Henry Purcell\, performed by Jul
 ie Comparini (voice) and Seth Demspey (lute). Julie will replace Christine
  who unfortunately is unable to join us in December. \n\nJulie Comparini 
 studied cognitive science and linguistics at the University of California-
 Berkeley and early music singing at the Hochschule für Künste in Bremen
 \, Germany. As a multimedia singer and performer\, she builds bridges betw
 een genres and disciplines.\nwww.juliecomparini.com/en/\n \nFree admissio
 n. Book will be available.\n Biographical Note:Jörn Bullerdiek is a human
  geneticist currently working as scientific advisor at the Genetic and Imm
 unology Testing Laboratory\, Alicante\, Spain. He is also a committed supp
 orter of interdisciplinary collaborations\, such as the one on which this 
 book is based.\nChristine Süßmuth studied Early Music at the University 
 of Arts in Bremen\, Germany. As a soloist and ensemble singer\, she knows 
 exactly what she is talking about when it comes to musical technique\, mus
 ic history\, and the joy of making music.\nDo you know why Johann Sebastia
 n Bach was considered light-fingered? Or what you could do with a lock of 
 Mozart's hair if you had it? These questions could lead you to an ancient 
 mystery: Why do humans enjoy producing musical notes\, as Darwin put it? I
 s this ability literally in our DNA\, and if so\, what is it doing there? 
 The authors sailed dangerously close to the Sirens' Rock and joined Orpheu
 s in meeting the sinister creatures of the underworld. They traced the pat
 h of sound waves from their source to the listener's brain and analyzed th
 e genealogy of the Bach family and the song of zebra finches. They visited
  "Scarborough Fair" to buy parsley\, sage\, rosemary\, and thyme. Eventual
 ly\, they took the ferry to the "Isle of Capri\," referencing Frank Sinatr
 a and the many other singers who have covered that song. Overall\, the res
 ult is an entertaining and informative analysis of what makes music so fas
 cinating and how this fascination is reflected in our genes from ancient E
 gypt to the present day.\n\nListing: https://librairieresonance.com/en/blo
 gs/evenements/discussion-why-music-is-genetic\nMore information: https://l
 ibrairieresonance.com/en/blogs/evenements/discussion-why-music-is-genetic
LOCATION:Librairie Résonance Bookstore\, 40 Beaubien East\, Montréal
STATUS:CONFIRMED
URL:https://librairieresonance.com/en/blogs/evenements/discussion-why-musi
 c-is-genetic
X-MONTREAL-LITERARY-SOURCE:librairie-resonance
END:VEVENT
